L("large-numbers-around-us","exact-and-approximate-values-and-product-patterns","Exact and approximate values, and patterns in products","The newspaper said 'about 5 lakh people came to the fair' while the gate counted 4,87,236.\n\"Which is right?\" asked Ravi.",
 ["An exact value is the precise number, such as 4,87,236. An approximate value is a rounded number close to it, such as about 5 lakh. We use approximate values when exactness is not needed.","To round to the nearest lakh, look at the digit in the ten-thousands place. 4,87,236 has 8 there, so it rounds up to 5,00,000.","Patterns in products appear with powers of 10: multiplying by 10 adds a zero, and 1 lakh times 10 is 10 lakh."],
 ["exact","approximate","round"],("Round 4,87,236 to the nearest lakh.",[("Look at the ten-thousands digit 8.","Digit."),("8 is 5 or more, so round up.","Up."),("The answer is 5,00,000.","Answer.")]),
 ("an approximate value is always exact.",["An approximation is close.","It is useful when exact is not needed."],["Exact vs close."]),
 ["Exact is precise, approximate is rounded.","Round using the next digit."],
 ["m|About 5 lakh is an|approximate value|exact value|zero|Round.","t|We round up if the next digit is 5 or more.|T|Yes.","m|4,87,236 to the nearest lakh|5,00,000|4,00,000|4,90,000|Up.","t|An approximate value is always exact.|F|Close.","m|1 lakh x 10 =|10 lakh|1 crore|1 thousand|Add zero.","s|1 crore = ___ lakh. Write the number.|100|Hundred.","m|Exact values are used when|precision matters|never|always rounded|Exact.","t|Multiplying by 10 adds a zero.|T|Yes."])
L("arithmetic-expressions","terms-swapping-grouping-and-removing-brackets","Terms, swapping and grouping, and removing brackets","Meena wrote 8 + 2 x 3 + 4 and counted its terms.\n\"Three terms,\" said her teacher.",
 ["A term is a part of an expression joined by plus or minus. In 8 plus 2 times 3 plus 4 the terms are 8, 2 times 3 and 4.","We can swap and group in addition and multiplication: 3 plus 5 equals 5 plus 3 and 2 times 3 times 5 can be grouped as (2 times 5) times 3.","Removing brackets: a plus sign before the bracket leaves signs unchanged, a minus sign changes every sign inside. 10 minus (3 plus 2) is 10 minus 3 minus 2, which is 5."],
 ["term","swap","bracket"],("Remove brackets in 10 minus (3 plus 2).",[("A minus before the bracket changes signs.","Rule."),("10 minus 3 minus 2.","Remove."),("The value is 5.","Answer.")]),
 ("subtraction can be swapped like addition.",["Addition can be swapped, subtraction cannot.","5 minus 3 is not 3 minus 5."],["Commutative error."]),
 ["Terms are joined by plus or minus.","Minus before a bracket flips signs."],
 ["m|Terms of 8 + 2x3 + 4|3|2|4|Count.","t|Addition can be swapped.|T|Yes.","m|10 - (3 + 2) =|5|9|15|Remove.","t|Subtraction can be swapped.|F|No.","m|A minus before a bracket|changes signs|keeps signs|deletes|Rule.","s|(2 x 5) x 3 =? Write only the number.|30|Group.","m|Terms are separated by|plus or minus|times|nothing|Sign.","t|Multiplication can be grouped.|T|Yes."])
L("a-peek-beyond-the-point","tenths-hundredths-and-units-of-measurement","Tenths, hundredths and units of measurement","Raju measured a pencil as 12 cm 5 mm.\n\"That is 12.5 cm,\" said his teacher.",
 ["When whole units are too big we use smaller units. One tenth is one of ten equal parts, written 0.1. One hundredth is one of a hundred equal parts, written 0.01.","Units give examples: 1 cm is 0.01 m, 1 mm is 0.1 cm and 1 paisa is 0.01 rupee. So 12 cm 5 mm is 12.5 cm.","In 3.47, the 4 is in the tenths place and the 7 is in the hundredths place."],
 ["tenth","hundredth","decimal"],("Write 12 cm 5 mm in centimetres.",[("5 mm is 0.5 cm.","Convert."),("12 plus 0.5 is 12.5.","Add."),("So 12.5 cm.","Answer.")]),
 ("0.1 is smaller than 0.01.",["0.1 is ten times 0.01.","Tenths are larger than hundredths."],["Place confusion."]),
 ["Tenths and hundredths are smaller units.","Decimals link units."],
 ["m|1 mm in cm|0.1 cm|1 cm|10 cm|Tenth.","t|1 cm is 0.01 m.|T|Yes.","m|In 3.47 the 4 is in the|tenths place|hundredths place|ones place|Place.","t|0.1 is smaller than 0.01.|F|Larger.","m|12 cm 5 mm =|12.5 cm|12.05 cm|125 cm|Convert.","s|1 paisa in rupees? Write 0.01.|0.01|Hundredth.","m|A tenth is one of|10 equal parts|100 equal parts|2 parts|Ten.","t|We use smaller units when whole units are too big.|T|Yes."])
L("expressions-using-letter-numbers","algebraic-modelling-and-simplifying-expressions","Algebraic modelling and simplifying expressions","A shopkeeper sold pencils at 3 rupees and erasers at 2 rupees.\n\"I can write the total as 3p + 2e,\" he said.",
 ["We model real situations using letter-numbers. If p pencils and e erasers are bought, the cost is 3p plus 2e.","We omit the multiplication sign: 3p means 3 times p. Like terms have the same letter and can be combined: 3p plus 5p is 8p. Unlike terms cannot be combined.","To simplify 2n plus 3 plus 4n minus 1, group like terms: 6n plus 2."],
 ["like terms","simplify","model"],("Simplify 2n + 3 + 4n - 1.",[("Group n terms: 2n plus 4n is 6n.","n terms."),("Group numbers: 3 minus 1 is 2.","Numbers."),("The answer is 6n plus 2.","Answer.")]),
 ("3p + 2e = 5pe.",["They are unlike terms.","They cannot be combined."],["Combining unlike terms."]),
 ["Like terms combine.","Unlike terms stay separate."],
 ["m|3p + 5p =|8p|15p|8|Like.","t|3p means 3 times p.|T|Yes.","m|2n + 4n =|6n|8n|6|Combine.","t|3p + 2e = 5pe.|F|Unlike.","m|Simplify 2n + 3 + 4n - 1|6n + 2|6n + 4|8n|Group.","s|5a - 2a = ? Write like 3a.|3a|Combine.","m|Like terms have the same|letter|number|colour|Same.","t|Letters can stand for numbers.|T|Yes."])
L("parallel-and-intersecting-lines","perpendicular-lines-and-drawing-parallels","Perpendicular lines and drawing parallel lines","Anu folded a sheet of paper twice and the creases made a right angle.\n\"They are perpendicular,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Two lines are perpendicular if they meet at a right angle of 90 degrees. A paper fold can make a perpendicular: fold a line onto itself.","Parallel lines never meet and stay the same distance apart. We can draw them with a set square and ruler by sliding one set square along another.","Folding paper in the same way twice gives parallel creases."],
 ["perpendicular","parallel","set square"],("How do we draw a line parallel to another?",[("Place the ruler along the line.","Ruler."),("Slide the set square along the ruler.","Slide."),("Draw along its edge.","Answer.")]),
 ("perpendicular lines meet at 45 degrees.",["They meet at 90 degrees.","That is a right angle."],["Wrong angle."]),
 ["Perpendicular lines make 90 degrees.","Parallel lines never meet."],
 ["m|Perpendicular lines meet at|90 degrees|45 degrees|0|Right.","t|Parallel lines never meet.|T|Yes.","m|We draw parallels with a|set square and ruler|fork|spoon|Tools.","t|Perpendicular lines meet at 45 degrees.|F|90.","m|Folding paper can make|a perpendicular|nothing|an animal|Fold.","s|A right angle is ___ degrees. Number.|90|Ninety.","m|Parallel lines stay|equally far apart|touching|crossing|Distance.","t|Two folds can make parallel creases.|T|Yes."])
L("number-play","parity-and-small-squares-in-grids","Parity and small squares in grids","Ravi saw that adding two even numbers always gave an even number.\n\"Is that always true?\" he asked.",
 ["Parity tells whether a whole number is even or odd. Even plus even is even, odd plus odd is even and even plus odd is odd.","The sum of the first n odd numbers is n squared. We can see this by arranging small squares in a grid.","Parity of expressions: 2n is always even and 2n plus 1 is always odd for any whole number n."],
 ["parity","even","odd"],("Is the sum of two odd numbers even or odd?",[("Take 3 and 5.","Example."),("3 plus 5 is 8.","Add."),("The sum is even.","Answer.")]),
 ("odd plus odd is odd.",["Odd plus odd is even.","Pairs make even numbers."],["Wrong parity."]),
 ["Parity means even or odd.","2n is even, 2n+1 is odd."],
 ["m|Odd + odd is|even|odd|zero|Parity.","t|2n is always even.|T|Yes.","m|Even + odd is|odd|even|zero|Mix.","t|Odd + odd is odd.|F|Even.","m|2n + 1 is always|odd|even|zero|Odd.","s|Sum of first 4 odd numbers? Write only the number.|16|Square.","m|Parity tells us if a number is|even or odd|big or small|round|Parity.","t|Grids of squares show patterns.|T|Yes."])
L("a-tale-of-three-intersecting-lines","constructing-triangles-and-triangle-inequality","Constructing triangles and the triangle inequality","Meena tried to make a triangle with sticks of 2, 3 and 7 cm and could not.\n\"The sides do not meet,\" she said.",
 ["We can construct a triangle when three sides are given using a compass: draw one side and swing arcs from its ends.","We can also construct it when two sides and the angle between them are given, or two angles and the side between them.","The triangle inequality says the sum of any two sides must be greater than the third. 2 plus 3 is less than 7, so no triangle exists."],
 ["construct","inequality","arc"],("Can sides 3, 4 and 5 make a triangle?",[("3 plus 4 is 7, more than 5.","Check."),("3 plus 5 is more than 4.","Check."),("Yes, they form a triangle.","Answer.")]),
 ("sides 2, 3 and 7 can form a triangle.",["2 plus 3 is less than 7.","The sides cannot meet."],["Ignoring inequality."]),
 ["Sum of two sides exceeds the third.","Arcs fix the third corner."],
 ["m|Sides 3, 4, 5|form a triangle|do not form|are parallel|Check.","t|Two sides must add to more than the third.|T|Yes.","m|Sides 2, 3, 7|cannot form a triangle|form one|are equal|Fail.","t|Sides 2, 3, 7 can form a triangle.|F|No.","m|To construct from three sides we use|arcs|only ruler|nothing|Compass.","s|3 + 4 compared with 5, bigger or smaller?|bigger|7>5.","m|Triangle inequality compares|sums of sides|colours|angles only|Sides.","t|We can construct with two sides and an angle.|T|Yes."])
L("working-with-fractions","fraction-problems-in-everyday-life","Problems involving fractions","A tank was 3/4 full and Ravi used 1/3 of the water.\n\"How much is left?\" asked Meena.",
 ["To solve fraction problems read carefully, decide whether to multiply or divide and write the operation. 'Of' means multiply.","1/3 of 3/4 is 1/3 times 3/4, which is 1/4. So he used 1/4 of the tank and 3/4 minus 1/4 is 1/2 of the tank is left.","Check that the answer is sensible: the amount left must be smaller than what we started with."],
 ["of","reciprocal","check"],("Find 1/3 of 3/4.",[("Of means multiply.","Of."),("1/3 times 3/4 is 3/12.","Multiply."),("3/12 is 1/4.","Answer.")]),
 ("'of' means add.",["Of means multiply.","1/2 of 8 is 4."],["Wrong operation."]),
 ["Of means multiply.","Check answers for sense."],
 ["m|1/3 of 3/4 =|1/4|1/12|4/7|Multiply.","t|'Of' means multiply.|T|Yes.","m|3/4 - 1/4 =|1/2|1/4|1|Subtract.","t|'Of' means add.|F|Multiply.","m|Amount left must be|smaller than start|bigger|zero|Sense.","s|1/2 of 8? Write only the number.|4|Half.","m|1/2 x 1/2 =|1/4|1|1/2|Half of half.","t|We should check our answers.|T|Yes."])
L("geometric-twins","conditions-that-guarantee-congruence","Conditions that guarantee congruence (SSS, SAS, ASA)","Raju cut two triangles from paper using the same three side lengths and they matched exactly.\n\"Three equal sides are enough,\" he said.",
 ["Two triangles are congruent if all matching sides and angles are equal, but we do not need all six. Three equal sides (SSS) are enough.","Two sides and the angle between them (SAS) are enough, and so are two angles and the side between them (ASA).","Two sides and an angle not between them (SSA) do not always guarantee congruence. Angles of an isosceles triangle opposite equal sides are equal."],
 ["SSS","SAS","ASA"],("Which conditions prove congruence?",[("SSS: three equal sides.","One."),("SAS: two sides and the included angle.","Two."),("ASA: two angles and the included side.","Three.")]),
 ("three equal angles always make congruent triangles.",["Equal angles may give different sizes.","Congruence needs side lengths."],["AAA error."]),
 ["SSS, SAS, ASA prove congruence.","Isosceles base angles are equal."],
 ["m|SSS stands for|three equal sides|three angles|none|Sides.","t|SAS needs the included angle.|T|Yes.","m|ASA uses|two angles and the side between|three sides|none|Angles.","t|Three equal angles guarantee congruence.|F|Not enough.","m|Base angles of an isosceles triangle are|equal|different|zero|Equal.","s|Minimum equal sides for SSS? Write only the number.|3|Three.","m|Congruent triangles have equal|size and shape|colour|name|Match.","t|SSA does not always work.|T|Yes."])
L("operations-with-integers","brahmagupta-rules-and-integer-multiplication-patterns","Brahmagupta's rules and multiplication patterns","Asha noticed that 3 x (-1), 2 x (-1), 1 x (-1) gave -3, -2, -1 and the next answer was 0.\n\"The pattern continues,\" she said.",
 ["Patterns explain integer multiplication. As the first number goes 3, 2, 1, 0, the answers of x (-1) go -3, -2, -1, 0, then (-1) x (-1) is 1.","Brahmagupta wrote rules centuries ago: a debt multiplied by a debt is a fortune, so negative times negative is positive.","Division follows from multiplication: if (-3) x 2 = -6 then (-6) divided by 2 is -3."],
 ["pattern","fortune","debt"],("Use a pattern to show (-1) x (-1).",[("2 x (-1) is -2.","Step."),("1 x (-1) is -1, then 0 x (-1) is 0.","Step."),("The next is (-1) x (-1) = 1.","Answer.")]),
 ("a debt times a debt is a debt.",["Brahmagupta: debt times debt is fortune.","Negative times negative is positive."],["Wrong rule."]),
 ["Patterns justify the rules.","Negative times negative is positive."],
 ["m|(-1) x (-1) =|1|-1|0|Pattern.","t|Brahmagupta gave sign rules.|T|Yes.","m|(-3) x 2 =|-6|6|-5|Negative.","t|A debt times a debt is a debt.|F|Fortune.","m|(-6) / 2 =|-3|3|-4|Divide.","s|(-2) x (-3) = ? Write only the number.|6|Positive.","m|Debt means|negative|positive|zero|Owe.","t|Division follows from multiplication.|T|Yes."])
L("finding-common-ground","lcm-through-prime-factorisation-and-patterns","Finding the LCM through prime factorisation","Two bells ring every 12 and 18 minutes and the teacher asked when they ring together.\n\"Use prime factors,\" said Meena.",
 ["Write each number as a product of primes: 12 is 2 x 2 x 3 and 18 is 2 x 3 x 3. For the LCM take each prime to its highest power: 2 x 2 x 3 x 3 is 36.","For the HCF take each common prime to its lowest power: 2 x 3 is 6.","A useful property: HCF times LCM of two numbers equals their product. 6 x 36 = 216 = 12 x 18."],
 ["LCM","highest power","product"],("Find the LCM of 12 and 18 using primes.",[("12 is 2 x 2 x 3.","Factor."),("18 is 2 x 3 x 3.","Factor."),("LCM is 2 x 2 x 3 x 3, which is 36.","Answer.")]),
 ("HCF x LCM is different from the product of the numbers.",["For two numbers they are equal.","6 x 36 equals 12 x 18."],["Wrong property."]),
 ["LCM uses highest powers.","HCF x LCM = product of two numbers."],
 ["m|LCM of 12 and 18|36|6|216|Primes.","t|HCF x LCM equals the product.|T|Yes.","m|HCF of 12 and 18|6|36|3|Lowest.","t|HCF x LCM differs from the product.|F|Equal.","m|18 as primes|2 x 3 x 3|2 x 9|3 x 6|Primes.","s|HCF of 6 and 9? Write only the number.|3|Common.","m|For LCM we take the|highest power|lowest power|none|High.","t|Bells ringing together is an LCM problem.|T|Yes."])
L("another-peek-beyond-the-point","estimating-decimal-products-and-quotients","Look before you leap: estimating decimal products and quotients","Meena multiplied 4.2 by 3.1 and got 130.2.\n\"That cannot be right,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Before multiplying decimals estimate the answer: 4.2 is about 4 and 3.1 is about 3, so the product is about 12. The answer 130.2 is clearly wrong, and the correct answer is 13.02.","To multiply, ignore the decimal points, multiply the numbers and then place the decimal so that the answer has as many decimal places as both numbers together.","To divide decimals, multiply both numbers by 10 or 100 to make the divisor a whole number."],
 ["estimate","decimal places","divisor"],("Estimate 4.2 x 3.1.",[("4.2 is about 4.","Round."),("3.1 is about 3.","Round."),("4 times 3 is about 12.","Answer.")]),
 ("4.2 x 3.1 is 130.2.",["The answer should be near 12.","The correct answer is 13.02."],["Misplaced decimal."]),
 ["Estimate first.","Count decimal places."],
 ["m|4.2 x 3.1 is about|12|120|1.2|Round.","t|Estimating catches decimal errors.|T|Yes.","m|4.2 x 3.1 =|13.02|130.2|1.302|Places.","t|4.2 x 3.1 is 130.2.|F|13.02.","m|2.5 x 2 =|5|50|0.5|Double.","s|0.5 x 0.5 = ? Write 0.25.|0.25|Quarter.","m|To divide decimals we make the divisor a|whole number|fraction|zero|Shift.","t|Decimal places add when multiplying.|T|Yes."])
L("connecting-the-dots","questions-statements-and-telling-tall-tales","Questions, statements and data that tell tall tales","A poster claimed 'nine out of ten doctors recommend this toothpaste'.\n\"Who did they ask?\" wondered Ravi.",
 ["Data begins with a question. We choose whom to ask and how, collect answers and make statements. Good questions are clear and fair.","Representative values such as the mean, median and mode summarise data. Graphs help us see it, but graphs can mislead if scales or ends are changed.","Always ask: Who collected this? How many were asked? Is the scale fair? This is how we spot tall tales."],
 ["data","representative value","misleading"],("Why can a graph mislead?",[("The scale may start at a high number.","Scale."),("Small differences look big.","Look."),("So we must check the axes.","Answer.")]),
 ("every statistic we read is fair.",["Some are misleading.","Check the source and scale."],["Trusting blindly."]),
 ["Ask clear questions.","Check graphs and sources."],
 ["m|Representative values include|mean, median, mode|colours|toys|Summary.","t|Graphs can mislead.|T|Yes.","m|To check a claim we ask|who was asked|nothing|no one|Check.","t|Every statistic is fair.|F|Not always.","m|Data starts with a|question|answer|graph|Start.","s|The middle value is the ___ . One word.|median|Median.","m|A fair scale|starts evenly|is hidden|is random|Fair.","t|Sample size matters.|T|Yes."])
L("constructions-and-tilings","tiling-with-regular-polygons","Tiling with regular polygons","Raju saw a floor covered with hexagonal tiles with no gaps.\n\"Tiling,\" said his father.",
 ["Tiling means covering a flat surface with shapes without gaps or overlaps. Regular tiles of one kind are possible only for equilateral triangles, squares and regular hexagons.","This works because the angles at a corner must add up to 360 degrees. Six triangles of 60 degrees, four squares of 90 degrees and three hexagons of 120 degrees each make 360.","A regular pentagon has angles of 108 degrees, which do not divide 360, so it cannot tile alone."],
 ["tiling","tessellation","corner"],("Why can squares tile a floor?",[("Each corner is 90 degrees.","Angle."),("Four corners make 360 degrees.","Sum."),("So there are no gaps.","Answer.")]),
 ("a regular pentagon can tile by itself.",["108 does not divide 360.","Gaps remain."],["Wrong claim."]),
 ["Angles at a point add to 360.","Triangles, squares, hexagons tile."],
 ["m|Angles at a point add to|360|180|90|Full turn.","t|Squares can tile a floor.|T|Yes.","m|Hexagon angle|120 degrees|90|60|Regular.","t|A regular pentagon tiles alone.|F|No.","m|Triangle angle in a tiling|60 degrees|90|120|Equilateral.","s|Squares meeting at a point? Write only the number.|4|4x90.","m|Tiling leaves no|gaps|colour|room|Gaps.","t|Tessellation means tiling.|T|Yes."])
L("finding-the-unknown","matchstick-patterns-and-frame-equations","Matchstick patterns and frame equations","Anu made triangles from matchsticks, adding two sticks for each new triangle.\n\"How many for 10 triangles?\" asked her friend.",
 ["Matchstick patterns give rules. A row of n triangles needs 2n plus 1 sticks: 3, 5, 7, 9 and so on. For 10 triangles we need 21 sticks.","To find how many triangles can be made with 31 sticks, solve 2n plus 1 equals 31, so 2n is 30 and n is 15.","A frame equation puts a box where the unknown goes: box plus 7 equals 12 gives box equals 5."],
 ["pattern","equation","unknown"],("How many triangles can 31 sticks make?",[("Rule is 2n plus 1.","Rule."),("2n plus 1 equals 31 gives 2n equals 30.","Solve."),("n is 15.","Answer.")]),
 ("a row of n triangles needs 3n sticks.",["It needs 2n plus 1.","Triangles share sides."],["Wrong rule."]),
 ["Patterns give equations.","Solve to find the unknown."],
 ["m|Sticks for 10 triangles|21|30|20|2n+1.","t|Frame equations use a box for the unknown.|T|Yes.","m|2n + 1 = 31 gives n =|15|30|16|Solve.","t|A row of n triangles needs 3n sticks.|F|2n+1.","m|Box + 7 = 12 gives box =|5|19|7|Subtract.","s|2n = 30, n = ? Write only the number.|15|Half.","m|Matchstick patterns follow a|rule|nothing|noise|Pattern.","t|Equations find unknown numbers.|T|Yes."])
L("finding-the-unknown","history-of-algebra-and-mending-mistakes","History of algebra and mending mistakes","Ravi found that his answer to 2x + 3 = 11 gave x = 7 and the check failed.\n\"Mind the mistake, mend the mistake,\" said his teacher.",
 ["Algebra grew from many places. Indian mathematicians like Brahmagupta and Aryabhata used symbols and rules for unknowns, and the word algebra comes from al-jabr, a term used by the Arab scholar al-Khwarizmi.","Common mistakes: forgetting to do the same on both sides, or mishandling signs. In 2x + 3 = 11 we subtract 3 to get 2x = 8, then divide by 2 to get x = 4.","We check by substituting back: 2 x 4 + 3 = 11, which is true."],
 ["algebra","Brahmagupta","check"],("Solve 2x + 3 = 11.",[("Subtract 3 from both sides: 2x = 8.","Step."),("Divide by 2: x = 4.","Step."),("Check: 2 x 4 + 3 is 11.","Answer.")]),
 ("2x + 3 = 11 gives x = 7.",["x = 4.","Subtract 3 then divide by 2."],["Skipping a step."]),
 ["Algebra has a long history.","Check every solution."],
 ["m|2x + 3 = 11 gives x =|4|7|8|Solve.","t|We check by substitution.|T|Yes.","m|Brahmagupta was an|Indian mathematician|American|Greek chef|India.","t|2x + 3 = 11 gives x = 7.|F|x = 4.","m|The word algebra comes from|al-jabr|ancient Rome|India only|Name.","s|2x = 8, x = ? Write only the number.|4|Half.","m|A common mistake is|changing one side only|checking|nothing|Balance.","t|Algebra helps solve for unknowns.|T|Yes."])
